# 4. Curriculum Design & Spatial-Area Step Scaling Law

Training multi-agent policies directly on full $ 50 \times 50 $ facility layouts leads to exploration collapse due to sparse initial reward signals. HEIST employs a 5-stage geometric curriculum that progressively expands facility dimensions while scaling step budgets proportionally.

## 4.1 Geometric Stage Geometry

The 5 curriculum stages expand map area $ A_k = W_k \times H_k $ smoothly while introducing security entities:

| Stage $ k $ | Map Size $(W \times H)$ | Total Area $ A_k $ | Guards | Cameras | Doors | Primary Learning Objective |
| :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :--- |
| **Stage 0** | $ 11 \times 11 $ | 121 tiles | 0 | 0 | 0 | Master basic causal chain (scout $\to$ hack $\to$ extract) |
| **Stage 1** | $ 17 \times 17 $ | 289 tiles | 1 | 0 | 1 | Master 1-guard patrol timing |
| **Stage 2** | $ 25 \times 25 $ | 625 tiles | 2 | 1 | 2 | Master single-camera vision cone evasion & hacking |
| **Stage 3** | $ 35 \times 35 $ | 1,225 tiles | 3 | 2 | 3 | Master multi-camera security coordination |
| **Stage 4** | $ 50 \times 50 $ | 2,500 tiles | 4 | 3 | 4 | Full benchmark facility extraction |

## 4.2 Formalization of Spatial Sample Invariance ($ \rho $)

In a 2D Partially Observable Markov Decision Process (Dec-POMDP) with local vision window size $ w_{\text{obs}} \times h_{\text{obs}} $, the spatial sample density $ \rho $ is defined as the expected number of environment transition steps collected per unit grid area:

$ \rho(T_k, A_k) = \frac{T_k}{A_k} \quad \left[ \frac{\text{environment steps}}{\text{grid tile}} \right] $

To achieve **Spatial Sample Invariance** across curriculum stages—ensuring policy gradient estimators (GAE) receive an identical density of observations per tile regardless of facility size—we require $ \rho $ to remain constant across all stages:

$ \rho(T_k, A_k) = \rho(T_0, A_0) \quad \forall k \in \{0, 1, 2, 3, 4\} $

where $ A_0 = 11 \times 11 = 121 \text{ tiles} $ is the Stage 0 baseline facility area.

## 4.3 Step Scaling Law Formula

Equating spatial sample densities $ \frac{T_k}{A_k} = \frac{T_0}{121} $ yields the exact step scaling law used by `scripts/train.zsh`:

$ T_k = T_0 \cdot \frac{W_k \cdot H_k}{121} $

### Optimal Multi-Stage Step Allocation ($ T_0 = 1,000,000 $)

Setting the Stage 0 baseline to the optimal convergence budget $ T_0 = 1,000,000 \text{ steps} $ ($ \rho^* \approx 8,264 \text{ steps/tile} $), the exact multi-stage step allocations are:

| Curriculum Stage $ k $ | Facility Area $ A_k $ | Area Scale Factor $ \frac{A_k}{121} $ | Optimal Step Budget ($ T_k $) | Cumulative Campaign Steps |
| :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: |
| **Stage 0** ($ 11 \times 11 $) | 121 | $ 1.000\times $ | **1,000,000 steps** (1.00M) | 1,000,000 steps |
| **Stage 1** ($ 17 \times 17 $) | 289 | $ 2.388\times $ | **2,388,429 steps** (2.39M) | 3,388,429 steps |
| **Stage 2** ($ 25 \times 25 $) | 625 | $ 5.165\times $ | **5,165,289 steps** (5.17M) | 8,553,718 steps |
| **Stage 3** ($ 35 \times 35 $) | 1,225 | $ 10.124\times $ | **10,123,966 steps** (10.12M) | 18,677,684 steps |
| **Stage 4** ($ 50 \times 50 $) | 2,500 | $ 20.661\times $ | **20,661,157 steps** (20.66M) | 39,338,841 steps |
| **Total Campaign** | — | **$ 39.338\times $** | **39,338,841 steps** (39.34M) | — |

This spatial scaling law mathematically guarantees that every curriculum stage maintains the exact same spatial sample coverage $ \rho \approx 8,264 \text{ steps / tile} $, providing a principled foundation for multi-stage MARL evaluation.
